\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

We describe the model configurations, evaluation benchmarks, and analytical framework used to measure how attention affects cross-architecture agreement at both aggregate and per-example levels.

\subsection{Model Zoo}\label{ssec:model_zoo}

We use the pretrained model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, which provides controlled comparisons across architectures and attention ratios. The zoo contains three linear recurrent architectures: GatedDeltaNet \citep{yang2024gateddeltanet}, RetNet \citep{sun2023retnet}, and HGRN2 \citep{qin2024hgrn2}. Each architecture is trained at five attention ratios: 0\% (pure recurrent), 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, and 25\%. We adopt the h$k$-1 notation from the original work, where one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ total layers: h3-1 corresponds to 25\% attention, h6-1 to 14.3\%, h12-1 to 7.7\%, and h24-1 to 4\%. All configurations are available at two scales: 340M and 1.3B parameters. Within each scale, all models share identical training data, tokenizer, and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. The zoo also includes a Transformer baseline (pure attention) at both scales.

\subsection{Task Taxonomy}\label{ssec:task_taxonomy}

We evaluate on 10 benchmarks grouped into four categories. This taxonomy was pre-registered before any analysis (decision record D003), not derived post hoc from observed results.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Retrieval}: LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada}, which requires predicting a final word from long-range context without reasoning or world knowledge.
    \item \textbf{Comprehension}: BoolQ, a reading comprehension task with yes/no answers.
    \item \textbf{Reasoning}: HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge \citep{clark2018arc}, WinoGrande, and PIQA. COPA ($n = 100$) serves as a supplementary reasoning benchmark.
    \item \textbf{Mixed}: ARC-Easy, MMLU, SciQ, and XStoryCloze, which combine retrieval, comprehension, and reasoning demands.
\end{itemize}

All evaluations use the lm-evaluation-harness framework \citep{gao2024lmeval} with default prompts and metrics for each benchmark.

\subsection{Convergence Metrics}\label{ssec:convergence_metrics}

We quantify cross-architecture agreement as a function of attention ratio. For each benchmark and attention ratio, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation $\sigma$ over the three architectures' accuracy scores. To measure the relationship between attention and convergence, we compute the Spearman rank correlation $\rho$ between attention ratio rank and $\sigma$ across the five attention configurations ($n = 5$). A negative $\rho$ indicates that higher attention ratios are associated with lower cross-architecture variance.

We also report the convergence ratio $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$, defined as the standard deviation under the pure-recurrent configuration divided by that under the highest attention ratio (25\%). Values greater than 1 indicate that attention reduces cross-architecture variance.

\subsection{Per-Example Decomposition}\label{ssec:per_example_decomposition}

To move beyond aggregate metrics, we decompose performance changes at the individual-example level on LAMBADA (5{,}153 examples). For each architecture, we compare the pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid and classify each example into one of three categories:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Helped}: correct in the hybrid but incorrect in the pure model.
    \item \textbf{Hurt}: correct in the pure model but incorrect in the hybrid.
    \item \textbf{Unchanged}: both models agree (both correct or both incorrect).
\end{itemize}

The net effect for each architecture is $|\text{helped}| - |\text{hurt}|$. To measure cross-architecture overlap in per-example effects, we compute the pairwise Jaccard similarity $J = |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ between the helped sets of two architectures and, separately, between their hurt sets. Low $J$ indicates that different architectures are affected on different subsets of examples.

\subsection{Statistical Methods}\label{ssec:statistical_methods}

All statistical tests are two-tailed unless otherwise noted.

\paragraph{Permutation test.} With only $n = 5$ attention ratios, we use an exact permutation test for $\rho$. All $5! = 120$ permutations of the attention ratio ranks are enumerated, and the two-tailed $p$-value is the fraction of permutations yielding $|\rho| \geq |\rho_{\text{obs}}|$.

\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.} We construct 95\% BCa (bias-corrected and accelerated) bootstrap confidence intervals for $\rho$ using 10{,}000 resamples at the architecture level. When the BCa correction degenerates at a boundary value, we report percentile confidence intervals instead.

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} We apply Bonferroni correction across six pre-registered benchmarks (D003). The four remaining benchmarks are corrected separately across all 10 benchmarks and reported as exploratory.

\paragraph{Within- and cross-architecture tests.} For within-architecture comparisons of per-example properties (e.g., token count of helped vs.\ hurt examples), we use the Mann-Whitney U test. For cross-architecture comparisons of per-example effect distributions, we use the Kruskal-Wallis test.

